Practical No. 2

Foreground and Background

Aim :- 

Manage foreground and background processes.

Theory :- 

Linux allows multitasking via jobs. 
Foreground jobs run interactively and block the terminal shell until complete. 
Background jobs execute silently in the background, 
keeping the CLI available for additional inputs.

Requirements:

•Linux
•WSL Terminal

Command explanation :-

a) Run a process in background using &

$ sleep 100 &

Appending an ampersand (&) at the end of a command instructs the shell to spawn it as a background job, outputting its background job number and PID immediately.

b) View background jobs using jobs

$ jobs

Lists active background jobs spawned by the current shell session, indicating their status (running, stopped).

c) Bring job to foreground using fg

$ fg %1

Moves the background job identified by number (e.g. job 1) back into the active interactive foreground state.

d) Suspend and resume processes using Ctrl + Z and bg

Press Ctrl + Z while job is running in foreground.

$ bg %1

Ctrl + Z immediately stops/suspends an ongoing foreground task. Executing bg %1 resumes that stopped task safely in the background.

• Execution :- 

sleep 100 &

jobs

fg %1

Ctrl + Z

bg %1
